{
  "task_id": "Q063",
  "answer": "Dedicated static content hosting assumes a representation can be stored once and reused by many callers without per-request application compute; personalized, uncacheable content violates exactly that premise, and the pattern's own evidence treats it as a rejection condition.\n\n1. It is a hard contraindication, not a tuning problem. The pattern card states the good-fit conditions as static/versioned content, a retained version covering the rollback window, controlled cacheability and purge, and owned origin protection, and lists \"Reject when content is personalized/uncacheable, when versioning/purge cannot meet freshness, or when edge configuration is unowned.\" Personalization removes the first condition and usually the second as well, so no amount of configuration makes the dedicated store appropriate.\n\n2. Cacheability is a content and authority contract, not a network location. A shared edge may reuse a response only when it is permitted to; the cache key, freshness policy and authorization boundary must all agree. User-specific responses cannot share a key, and an authorization header is not a complete design for private edge caching, because explicit cache directives can alter reuse rules. The named hazard is explicit: \"A misclassified response can serve one user's private data to another.\" For uncacheable content the reuse decision is always \"no\", so the store/edge is on the path without ever being able to answer from it.\n\n3. Freshness and purge cannot repair it. Changing, deleting or restricting origin content does not invalidate cached copies; purges need propagation time, and CDN purges do not clear browser caches. A purge request without completion evidence is not a proven revocation bound. For content that changes per request, any cached copy is already wrong before a purge could land, and the private-content failure mode (public-to-private visibility change) becomes a distributed-revocation problem rather than a cache problem.\n\n4. The economics invert. Static hosting pays off by serving by URL with no per-request application compute, trading that for storage, egress, purge cost and a retained-version rollback path. Personalized content still requires per-request key construction, an authorization decision and an origin reach on every request, so the dedicated layer no longer bypasses the origin it was meant to protect. It then adds a second version authority and storage/egress/purge cost on top of full origin load - negative value rather than a reduced hit rate.\n\n5. The guidance points elsewhere. When content changes per request or the publication pipeline cannot be governed, a dynamic origin, self-hosted files or edge compute may be preferable; and a direct origin or a private cache may be preferable when every response is user-specific or the purge contract cannot be enforced. Edge caching is a fit only when the response is shareable and the key, freshness and authorization boundary are explicit.",
  "citations": [
    "tycho.page.4ee21701c36640119917d0bb900f9894#overview",
    "tycho.page.840c65c6698344ba8ed13f1f10038b5c#overview",
