{
  "task_id": "Q083",
  "answer": "A p95 is a quantile of an empirical distribution over individual request observations, so a checkout comparison needs a model that individuates requests. A fluid (stock-and-flow) model carries only aggregate rates and levels, so it contains no distribution to take a percentile of. The book's worked example states the case directly: at 20 requests/second offered against 24 requests/second capacity, a stock-and-flow model gives utilization 0.833 and a stable backlog, but it \"cannot show the p95 latency of successful requests, because it has no individual requests.\" If the decision is \"which candidate meets a 1.5-second p95 constraint,\" that model cannot answer it. The discrete-event model is required \"not because it is more sophisticated but because the decision depends on a distribution that the coarser model does not contain.\" The same page frames this as resolution, not a maturity ladder: stock-and-flow is cheap and good for conservation and backlog growth but \"does not represent individual request latency,\" whereas discrete-event \"represents individual requests and event ordering\" and \"can be wrong at a finer resolution.\"\n\nRequest-level events are necessary rather than merely a finer average for three reasons. First, population and outcome definition: a percentile is a value of a distribution, not a single request's maximum, and computing \"p95 of successful requests\" requires declaring the observation window, whether timed-out requests are included, how sampling works, and whether successful, rejected and failed requests share the same population. Those are per-request attributes that a fluid model does not have. Second, the tail-generating mechanism is per-request: queue wait, service-time variance, deadlines and retries interact. Timeout handling is per-attempt - a deadline is arrival time plus the configured timeout, a timed-out attempt releases its server and schedules a retry with a new deadline, and a request with three attempts can spend up to three timeouts plus two backoffs in the system. Whether a completion at t=1.0 or a timeout at t=1.0 is processed first decides success versus failure, so the ordering of events carries the outcome, not just the duration. Third, composition: percentiles do not add; parent p95 is not the sum of child p95s, and correlated slow dependencies (shared database, GC, congestion) mean a composed request's tail cannot be reconstructed from per-hop aggregates.\n\nThe boundary is decision-driven. Where the observable is a quantile over individuated requests, the fluid model is insufficient and the answer changes with detail. Where the decision is only \"does the backlog grow when offered load exceeds capacity?\", the stock-and-flow model is sufficient and an event model would add cost and verification burden without changing the answer. A mean is not a substitute in the p95 case, because the comparison turns precisely on dispersion that an averaging model removes.",
  "citations": [
    "tycho.page.d860bc57cdfe6c6163912819f7679d17#worked-example-why-the-checkout-comparison-needs-events",
    "tycho.page.d860bc57cdfe6c6163912819f7679d17#abstraction-is-a-choice-about-resolution",
    "tycho.page.45935212b3b7476d8a163520f065b825#1-one-percentile-cannot-describe-a-distributed-request",
    "tycho.page.f914586e44ea034deaf2710c570328fc#timeout-lifecycle"
  ],
  "abstain": false,
