{
  "task_id": "Q090",
  "answer": "The V12 quadratic screen approximates a Sobol' analysis; it is not one. A full Sobol'/pick-freeze analysis uses paired model evaluations to build an ANOVA decomposition of the model's variance: the first-order index is the share of output variance V explained by input i alone, and the total-order index includes every interaction involving i. That estimator is unbiased only under its assumptions and is noisy at small sample sizes, and the page states that a full pick-freeze analysis is expensive for a discrete-event model.\n\nThe V12 screen instead runs one model run per row for 48 input rows drawn from the declared distributions, fits a quadratic surface in standardized input coordinates, decomposes the fitted variance into main effects plus a single interaction term, and reports first-order and total-order shares. Its indices therefore describe an approximated quadratic response surface over the sampled range, not a fully estimated decomposition of the simulator's variance.\n\nConsequences of that substitution, per the page: the screen assumes a quadratic surface is adequate over the sampled range and that standardization is meaningful; it requires independent inputs, so the engine rejects a correlated screen and correlated inputs are propagated only by the separate Monte Carlo run; and the failure modes include \"quadratic overreach\" from extrapolating beyond the sampled range, plus reporting indices without error. For candidate B the fitted screen gives arrival rate first-order 0.361 / total-order 0.464 and service mean 0.536 / 0.639; the first-order shares sum to 0.897, leaving about 0.103 for interaction and unexplained variation. The page explicitly warns that this does not prove service time is more important in production - under the declared distributions and the quadratic approximation it only explains more of the fitted variance. The sensitivity scenario is converged at 0.00016 relative error yet is still labeled a screening approximation, while a separate two-replication stress run at 0.032 relative error is marked insufficient-sample.\n\nIn short: full Sobol' = paired-evaluation, assumption-bounded estimate of model variance shares; V12 screen = cheap 48-row quadratic response-surface fit whose \"indices\" are fitted-surface shares, restricted to independent inputs and not a variance decomposition of the model itself.",
  "citations": [
    "tycho.page.7a8c030b04dc4f4809d4943e9be47ede#the-v12-screen",
    "tycho.page.7a8c030b04dc4f4809d4943e9be47ede#first-order-and-total-order-sensitivity",
    "tycho.page.7a8c030b04dc4f4809d4943e9be47ede#failure-modes"
  ],
  "abstain": false,
  "token_usage": {
    "total": null,
    "estimated": true
  }
}

{
  "task_id": "Q091",
  "answer": "In a V12 decision record a hard constraint is a gating condition a candidate must satisfy, while a soft constraint is only a preference that can be traded. A candidate that fails a hard constraint is eliminated outright, even if it is best on every other metric: the decision-record contract says that if candidate C exceeds a declared cost ceiling it is eliminated even though it has the highest success rate, and in the worked example C is eliminated by the hard cost and p95-latency constraints while A and B pass and are then compared. The record's decision tree encodes the same asymmetry: 'Hard constraints pass?' - No - 'Eliminate candidate'; only survivors are compared on success and cost, and trade-offs happen there. So a failed hard constraint removes the candidate from consideration rather than costing it points, whereas soft preferences are traded off among surviving candidates. Hard, soft and non-quantitative constraints are kept in separate fields precisely so a weighted composite score cannot hide or trade away a hard constraint. A non-quantitative constraint the model cannot measure (for example operability) does not eliminate a candidate; if it remains unverified the record keeps the recommendation conditional and records reversal triggers, which is a different treatment from both a failed hard constraint and an ordinary soft preference.",
  "citations": [
    "tycho.page.c7a43944f1d41e810eec0d17ec33b85a#hard-constraints-versus-preferences",
    "tycho.page.3cfd08ee56330be9b7a2d37975c3fe26#a-decision-tree"
  ],
  "abstain": false,
